{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_tze35lpffaijpcgp5yzoe7dh23fehdzsklxd73bssdgsfy2bfkfq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Verification complete — no divergence found\n\n`cargo check` was clean from the start, so no compile fixes were needed, and **no change to `translation/src/lib.rs` was required**: the translation is behaviourally identical to the C for everything I could throw at it.\n\n### Phase A — surface mapped (artifacts in `translation/`)\n\n| file | content |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` | 1 exported symbol (`parse_number`). C↔Rust `nm -D` diff **empty**. Both `.so`s import the same 4 libc functions (`malloc`/`free`/`memcpy`/`strtod`), confirming the Rust really routes allocation and conversion through libc. `c_src` has one TU and it is fully translated — nothing was skipped, so nothing had to be newly translated. |\n| `ERRORS.md` | 15 rows grepped out of the C: 4 distinct error returns (E1–E4) with 6 enumerated sub-triggers, 4 boundary rows, 1 documented missing-check/UB row. |\n| `CONFIGS.md` | 35 rows over 12 axes derived from the C's own `if`/`switch` structure (there are no feature flags or `#ifdef` modes), plus 6 cross-cutting matrices. |\n\n### Phases B/C/D — all rows pass, under all 6 configurations\n\n`./verify.sh` builds the C `.so`, then for each of `{default, --no-default-features, --all-features} × {debug, release}` re-checks symbol parity (`comm -23` → empty), `ldd -r` (0 undefined), and runs the 68-test suite. Both libraries are only ever reached via `dlopen`/`dlsym`, so the `#[unsafe(no_mangle)]` wrapper is under test. Each case compares the return, `item->type`/`valueint`/`valuedouble` (as raw `u64` bits, so `-0.0` and NaN payloads can't match by accident), all four `parse_buffer` fields, and the input bytes.\n\nRoughly **4.4M differential calls per configuration (~26M total), 0 divergences** — including exhaustively all 256 leading bytes, all 3 615 strings of length 1–3 and all 50 625 of length 4 over the accepted alphabet, all 15⁵ of length 5, all 7⁷ of length 7 over one byte per switch arm, all 2¹⁶ byte pairs × lengths × offsets, and 2M randomized cases.\n\n### Two test bugs found and fixed (never the C)\n\nMy own expectations were wrong twice; C and Rust agreed both times:\n- `\"2147483646.999999999\"` isn't representable in binary64 (spacing ≈4.8e-7 near 2³¹), so it rounds **up** to `2147483647.0` and saturates to `INT_MAX`.\n- `\"0x10\"` **parses as `0`, consuming 1 byte** — the scanner filters `x` out before `strtod`, so libc's hex-float path is unreachable.\n\n### Sensitivity proof (`./mutate.sh`)\n\nA green suite proves nothing unless it can fail, so I planted 19 deliberate bugs:\n- **16/16 behaviour-changing mutations caught** (dropping `E`/`+`/`.` from the accepted set; leaking `x` or space; wrong offset advance; removed NULL check; wrong `cJSON_Number`; wrong return on parse error; `<=` scan bound; short `malloc`; either saturation bound off by one; `floor` instead of truncate; missing `valueint` write; missing NUL).\n- **3 correctly *not* caught because they're provably unobservable** — `>=`→`>` at `INT_MAX`, `<=`→`<` at `INT_MIN` (the `else` branch's truncation yields the same value at those exact points), and never setting `has_decimal_point` (the loop rewrites `.` to a hard-coded `.`, a no-op). Documented as proofs, not gaps.\n\n`src/lib.rs` restored byte-for-byte afterwards (all 15 sites re-verified, still 230 lines).\n\n### C quirks deliberately preserved\n\nNo `item == NULL` check (`lib.c:92` writes unconditionally — replicated; not differentially testable since a segfault can't be compared); `decimal_point` hard-coded to `.`; `length` rather than a NUL bounds the scan; `offset` advances by `after_end - number_c_string`, not the scan length (so `\"1e\"` consumes 1 of 2 scanned bytes).\n\n`c_src/` is unmodified apart from the `build/` directory the instructions asked for. Full write-up in `translation/VERIFICATION.md`.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: I also dispatched an independent line-by-line audit of the C vs Rust as a redundant cross-check, and it hasn't reported back yet — everything above is from my own verification, and I'll fold in anything the audit turns up when it lands."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":794,"cache_read_input_tokens":152078,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":794,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9357c047-74df-4249-854b-1bf85b7adbcd","uuid":"f1b155ad-38ea-40bb-9a8e-81fe5298b6e7","timestamp":"2026-08-27T13:05:05.650Z"}
