.rs\n   139 tests/heavy_exhaustive.rs\n   850 tests/phase_b_valid.rs\n   725 tests/phase_c_errors.rs\n   260 tests/phase_d_matrix.rs\n    22 tests/smoke.rs\n   399 tests/common/mod.rs\n  3207 total\n\n=== c_src untouched (sources) ===\n-rw-r--r--. 1 scheschb amazon  406 Aug 27 12:43 ../c_src/CMakeLists.txt\n-rw-r--r--. 1 scheschb amazon  892 Aug 27 12:43 ../c_src/include/lib.h\n-rw-r--r--. 1 scheschb amazon 3202 Aug 27 12:43 ../c_src/src/lib.c\n\n=== symbol diff ===\n0\n=== cargo check --all-targets ===\n    Checking driver v0.1.0 (/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-DKQYPQ/translation)\n    Finished `dev` profile [unoptimized + debuginfo] target(s) in 0.04s","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_jdiwdnhjxyob3srrdca6pi2iny6ileri2w2kt3w5cisqjbbgv5bq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The independent audit came back and it **overturned my earlier \"no divergence\" conclusion**. I verified its finding myself, fixed it, and re-ran everything.\n\n## One real divergence — found, verified, fixed\n\n**Path:** `item == NULL`. The C never null-checks `item` (`lib.c:92` stores straight through the parameter). I had written this off in `ERRORS.md` row M1 as \"UB, can't compare a crash\" — that was wrong. *Which* fatal signal is perfectly comparable if you run each call in a child process.\n\nI reproduced it with a `SA_SIGINFO` handler:\n\n| artifact | before fix | after fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| C `libdriver.so` (reference) | SIGSEGV(11), `si_addr=0x8`, silent | unchanged |\n| Rust **release** | SIGSEGV(11), `si_addr=0x8` | unchanged |\n| Rust **debug** | **SIGABRT(6)** + `panicked at src/lib.rs:200: null pointer dereference occurred` | SIGSEGV(11), `si_addr=0x8` |\n| Rust release + `-C debug-assertions=on` | **SIGABRT(6)** + panic message | SIGSEGV(11), `si_addr=0x8` |\n\n**Cause:** written as the place expression `(*item).valuedouble = number`, rustc emits a null-pointer-dereference UB check whenever `-C debug-assertions` is on — and Cargo's `dev` profile enables that by default, so the shipped debug `.so` had it.\n\n**Fix** (`src/lib.rs`, new `item_store!` macro): route all five stores to `*item` through `core::ptr::addr_of_mut!` + `core::ptr::write`. `addr_of_mut!` computes the field address *without* dereferencing, and `ptr::write` is the same plain non-volatile store the C does. I chose this over `[profile.dev] debug-assertions = false` because it's a code-level fix that holds however the crate is built. `si_addr=0x8` also confirms store order still matches the C (`valuedouble` first).\n\n## What I had wrong, and why\n\nTwo process errors on my part, worth naming:\n\n1. **I declared the earlier phases complete while a check I had initiated was still outstanding.** I shouldn't have summarized a verdict of \"no divergence found\" with the audit in flight — the headline was wrong for the ~13 minutes before it returned.\n2. **I let a self-authored exclusion stand unchallenged.** `ERRORS.md` M1 said \"not differentially testable\"; I wrote that, then treated it as a fact rather than re-examining it. The exclusion was the bug's hiding place. A signal-comparing test was straightforward once I actually tried.\n\nM1 is now a genuine differential test (`m1_item_null_produces_the_same_fatal_signal`), and I confirmed it is sensitive by reverting the fix: it fails with `C uses signal Some(11), Rust Some(6)`.\n\n## Re-verified after the fix\n\n- **`verify.sh`**: 6 configurations (`{default, --no-default-features, --all-features} × {debug, release}`) — symbol diff empty, `ldd -r` clean, 65 tests pass, plus heavy sweeps: ~4.4M differential calls per config (~26M total), 0 divergences.\n- **`mutate.sh`**: now **19/19** behaviour-changing mutations caught (added guards for the `type` write, a swapped-field write, and reverting `item_store!`). The same 3 remain provably unobservable (`>=`→`>` at `INT_MAX`, `<=`→`<` at `INT_MIN`, `has_decimal_point` — the loop rewrites `.` to a hard-coded `.`).\n\nAlso confirmed independently: struct ABI (`cJSON` 16/8 at 0,4,8; `parse_buffer` 32/8 at 0,8,16,24) and both saturation thresholds are bit-exact, so Rust's saturating `as c_int` never differs from C's truncation, and NaN is unreachable (`n`/`a` are filtered by `default:` before `strtod`).\n\nThe audit independently confirmed items 1–10 and 12 equivalent, including extracting the accepted byte set from all four binaries via `malloc` interposition (identical 15 elements) and guard-page checks that neither implementation reads past `length`. `c_src/` sources are untouched. Full write-up in `translation/VERIFICATION.md`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1070,"cache_read_input_tokens":191554,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1070,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9357c047-74df-4249-854b-1bf85b7adbcd","uuid":"7bd1c61f-a30d-4b69-846d-5c985005ab5a","timestamp":"2026-08-27T13:17:59.574Z"}
{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":2242438,"num_turns":82,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"9357c047-74df-4249-854b-1bf85b7adbcd","total_cost_usd":13.123261750000001,"usage":{"input_tokens":152,"cache_creation_input_tokens":152872,"cache_read_input_tokens":6894517,"output_tokens":94467,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":21476},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":152872},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"global.anthropic.claude-opus-5[1m]":{"inputTokens":232,"outputTokens":177087,"cacheReadInputTokens":11769016,"cacheCreationInputTokens":449667,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":13.123261750000001,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"bedrock","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01QmqnvR9VHnb7n1ossc1Pk7","tool_input":{"command":"rm -rf /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-DKQYPQ/translation/target/abi-probe && ls /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-DKQYPQ/translation && echo \"--- final suite ---\" && timeout 600 cargo test --offline 2>&1 | grep 'test result'","description":"Clean probe dir and confirm suite green"}}],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"not_first_party","subagent_stats":{"spawned":1,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":1},"started_in_background":1,"max_depth":1,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":1,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{"general-purpose":1}},"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"## Verification complete — no divergence found\n\n`cargo check` was clean from the start, so no compile fixes were needed, and **no change to `translation/src/lib.rs` was required**: the translation is behaviourally identical to the C for everything I could throw at it.\n\n### Phase A — surface mapped (artifacts in `translation/`)\n\n| file | content |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` | 1 exported symbol (`parse_number`). C↔Rust `nm -D` diff **empty**. Both `.so`s import the same 4 libc functions (`malloc`/`free`/`memcpy`/`strtod`), confirming the Rust really routes allocation and conversion through libc. `c_src` has one TU and it is fully translated — nothing was skipped, so nothing had to be newly translated. |\n| `ERRORS.md` | 15 rows grepped out of the C: 4 distinct error returns (E1–E4) with 6 enumerated sub-triggers, 4 boundary rows, 1 documented missing-check/UB row. |\n| `CONFIGS.md` | 35 rows over 12 axes derived from the C's own `if`/`switch` structure (there are no feature flags or `#ifdef` modes), plus 6 cross-cutting matrices. |\n\n### Phases B/C/D — all rows pass, under all 6 configurations\n\n`./verify.sh` builds the C `.so`, then for each of `{default, --no-default-features, --all-features} × {debug, release}` re-checks symbol parity (`comm -23` → empty), `ldd -r` (0 undefined), and runs the 68-test suite. Both libraries are only ever reached via `dlopen`/`dlsym`, so the `#[unsafe(no_mangle)]` wrapper is under test. Each case compares the return, `item->type`/`valueint`/`valuedouble` (as raw `u64` bits, so `-0.0` and NaN payloads can't match by accident), all four `parse_buffer` fields, and the input bytes.\n\nRoughly **4.4M differential calls per configuration (~26M total), 0 divergences** — including exhaustively all 256 leading bytes, all 3 615 strings of length 1–3 and all 50 625 of length 4 over the accepted alphabet, all 15⁵ of length 5, all 7⁷ of length 7 over one byte per switch arm, all 2¹⁶ byte pairs × lengths × offsets, and 2M randomized cases.\n\n### Two test bugs found and fixed (never the C)\n\nMy own expectations were wrong twice; C and Rust agreed both times:\n- `\"2147483646.999999999\"` isn't representable in binary64 (spacing ≈4.8e-7 near 2³¹), so it rounds **up** to `2147483647.0` and saturates to `INT_MAX`.\n- `\"0x10\"` **parses as `0`, consuming 1 byte** — the scanner filters `x` out before `strtod`, so libc's hex-float path is unreachable.\n\n### Sensitivity proof (`./mutate.sh`)\n\nA green suite proves nothing unless it can fail, so I planted 19 deliberate bugs:\n- **16/16 behaviour-changing mutations caught** (dropping `E`/`+`/`.` from the accepted set; leaking `x` or space; wrong offset advance; removed NULL check; wrong `cJSON_Number`; wrong return on parse error; `<=` scan bound; short `malloc`; either saturation bound off by one; `floor` instead of truncate; missing `valueint` write; missing NUL).\n- **3 correctly *not* caught because they're provably unobservable** — `>=`→`>` at `INT_MAX`, `<=`→`<` at `INT_MIN` (the `else` branch's truncation yields the same value at those exact points), and never setting `has_decimal_point` (the loop rewrites `.` to a hard-coded `.`, a no-op). Documented as proofs, not gaps.\n\n`src/lib.rs` restored byte-for-byte afterwards (all 15 sites re-verified, still 230 lines).\n\n### C quirks deliberately preserved\n\nNo `item == NULL` check (`lib.c:92` writes unconditionally — replicated; not differentially testable since a segfault can't be compared); `decimal_point` hard-coded to `.`; `length` rather than a NUL bounds the scan; `offset` advances by `after_end - number_c_string`, not the scan length (so `\"1e\"` consumes 1 of 2 scanned bytes).\n\n`c_src/` is unmodified apart from the `build/` directory the instructions asked for. Full write-up in `translation/VERIFICATION.md`.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: I also dispatched an independent line-by-line audit of the C vs Rust as a redundant cross-check, and it hasn't reported back yet — everything above is from my own verification, and I'll fold in anything the audit turns up when it lands.","ttft_ms":2825,"ttft_stream_ms":2662,"time_to_request_ms":39,"type":"result","duration_ms":1318965,"uuid":"6d